HTML5 — Basic HTML Tags & Attributes

1. What is HTML?

HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language.

HTML is used to create and structure web pages.

HTML is not a programming language. It is a markup language.

Example:

<h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
<p>This is my first web page.</p>
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Basic HTML Structure

Every HTML page generally follows this structure:

<!DOCTYPE html>

<html>
    <head>
        <title>My First Web Page</title>
    </head>

    <body>
        <h1>Welcome to my Website</h1>
        <p>This is my first HTML page.</p>
    </body>
</html>
Structure Explanation
<!DOCTYPE html>
        ↓
    HTML5 Document
        ↓
     <html>
       /   \
   <head> <body>
     ↓       ↓
  Page Info  Visible Content
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. <!DOCTYPE html>
Definition

<!DOCTYPE html> tells the browser that the document is written using HTML5.

Syntax
<!DOCTYPE html>
Important Point
It is a declaration, not a normal HTML tag.
It should be the first line of an HTML document.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. <html> Tag
Definition

The <html> element is the root element of an HTML document.

All HTML content is written inside <html>.

Syntax
<html>
    ...
</html>
Example
<html>
    <head>
    </head>

    <body>
    </body>
</html>
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. <head> Tag
Definition

The <head> contains information about the webpage.

The content inside <head> is generally not displayed directly on the webpage.

It can contain:

<title>
CSS
JavaScript
Meta information
Favicon
Example
<head>
    <title>Student Management System</title>
</head>
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. <title> Tag
Definition

The <title> tag defines the title of the webpage.

The title is normally displayed in the browser tab.

Example
<title>My First Web Page</title>

Browser tab:

My First Web Page
Important

<title> and <h1> are different.

<title>My Website</title>

→ Browser tab

<h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>

→ Visible webpage heading
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

7. <body> Tag
Definition

The <body> contains the visible content of a webpage.

It can contain:

Headings
Paragraphs
Images
Links
Lists
Tables
Forms
Buttons
Example
<body>
    <h1>Welcome</h1>
    <p>Welcome to my website.</p>
</body>
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

8. Heading Tags

HTML provides six heading tags:

<h1>Heading 1</h1>
<h2>Heading 2</h2>
<h3>Heading 3</h3>
<h4>Heading 4</h4>
<h5>Heading 5</h5>
<h6>Heading 6</h6>
Heading Hierarchy
<h1> → Most important heading
<h2> → Subheading
<h3> → Sub-subheading
<h4>
<h5>
<h6> → Lowest heading level
Example
<h1>Student Management System</h1>

<h2>Student Information</h2>

<h3>Personal Details</h3>
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

9. <p> Tag
Definition

<p> stands for Paragraph.

It is used to display a paragraph of text.

Syntax
<p>Text goes here</p>
Example
<p>HTML is used to create web pages.</p>

<p>I am learning frontend development.</p>
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

10. <a> Tag
Definition

<a> stands for Anchor.

It is used to create a hyperlink.

Syntax
<a href="URL">Link Text</a>
Example
<a href="https://www.google.com/">Google</a>

The user sees:

Google

When the user clicks it, Google opens.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

11. href Attribute

href stands for Hypertext Reference.

It specifies the destination of a link.

Example
<a href="https://www.google.com/">Google</a>

Here:

<a>                         → Tag
href                        → Attribute
"https://www.google.com/"   → Attribute value
Google                      → Link text
Another Example
<a href="Home.html">Home</a>

This can be used to navigate to another HTML page in your project.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

12. Internal Link

An internal link points to another page/file within your website.

Example project:

Website
│
├── index.html
├── Home.html
├── About.html
└── Contact.html

HTML:

<a href="Home.html">Home</a>
<a href="About.html">About</a>
<a href="Contact.html">Contact</a>
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

13. External Link

An external link points to another website.

Example:

<a href="https://www.google.com/">Google</a>

<a href="https://www.youtube.com/">YouTube</a>
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

14. <img> Tag
Definition

The <img> tag is used to display an image on a webpage.

Syntax
<img src="image-path" alt="image description">
Example
<img src="Images/shirt.png" alt="Blue shirt">
15. src Attribute

src stands for Source.

It specifies the location/path of the image.

Example:

<img src="Images/shirt.png">

Here:

src → Attribute
Images/shirt.png → Image path
Example Project
Website
│
├── index.html
│
└── Images
    └── shirt.png

HTML:

<img src="Images/shirt.png">
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

16. alt Attribute

alt stands for Alternative Text.

It provides a description of an image.

Example:

<img src="Images/shirt.png" alt="Blue shirt">
Why use alt?
Helps when an image cannot load.
Improves accessibility.
Screen readers can read the description.
Provides useful information about the image.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

17. HTML Comments

Comments are written using:

<!-- This is a comment -->

The browser does not display comments on the webpage.

Example
<!-- This is the main heading -->

<h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
Multiple-line comment
<!--
    This is a
    multi-line comment
-->
Uses of Comments
Explain code
Add notes
Temporarily disable code
Help other developers understand the code
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

18. HTML Tags vs Attributes

This is an important concept.

Tag
<a>

a is the tag name.

Attribute
href="https://www.google.com/"

href is an attribute.

Complete Example
<a href="https://www.google.com/">Google</a>

Breakdown:

<a>                         → Tag
href                        → Attribute
"https://www.google.com/"   → Attribute Value
Google                      → Content
</a>                        → Closing Tag
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

19. Opening and Closing Tags

Most HTML elements have an opening and closing tag.

Example:

<p>Hello World</p>
<p>           → Opening tag
Hello World   → Content
</p>          → Closing tag

Another example:

<h1>Welcome</h1>
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

20. Empty/Void Elements

Some HTML elements don't require a closing tag.

Example:

<img src="shirt.png" alt="Shirt">

<img> is a void element.

Other common void elements include:

<br>
<hr>
<img>
<input>
<meta>
<link>